# The parts and their factors, resolved all the way to a layer that is actually here. One row per
# part whose notation names a document in this corpus and whose layer is in that document.
#
# One resolution, written once
#   Every question about composition (convert the parts, walk the composition, find the bottom,
#   weaken a coupling) starts by turning a notation into a filing, and a filing plus a layer into a
#   row. Written once, the rule for resolving is one thing that can be corrected in one place.
#
# A missing row is a reference to nothing
#   A row present in composition/part_references.sqlc and missing from here is a reference to
#   nothing, and the difference between the two relations is exactly what that rule finds. The rule
#   is an anti-join rather than a `NOT EXISTS`, because "the parts that resolve" is the relation
#   worth naming and "the parts that do not" is the rest.
#
# Resolution rests on what a document says about itself
#   `filing_identity` says which document claims which notation, and `asserted_by` records who said
#   it. Filled in as "the reader, from the filename", that would be a guess dressed as data; these
#   are read out of the documents.
#
# `pm.layer` directly, not `layers/every_layer.sqlc`
#   The last join only asks whether the layer this reference names exists.
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` lists every layer and is kept for denominators. Composed here, it
#   would end up among the relations most of the checks compose, and any law asking what a rule
#   reaches would then hold without testing anything, because that list reaches everything.
#   `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` states the rule and enforces it over what every relation composes.
-- pm.part joined through pm.filing_identity to pm.layer.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
       p.part_filing AS part_notation,
       fi.filing     AS part_filing,
       p.part_layer,
       p.part_regime,
       p.factor_low, p.factor_mode, p.factor_high, p.factor_absent, p.factor_derivation,
       p.factor_state
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/part_references.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.notation = p.part_filing
JOIN pm.layer l  ON l.filing = fi.filing AND l.layer = p.part_layer
